One thing that I would consider important "pre-inserted" information
would be a list of attributes, (perhaps, or learnable:) how to obtain
them, and what usefullness they have.  How to know when you've
obtained proper attributes to make passage to the next areas
relatively safe, or when to reconsider your progress in favor of
acquiring better characteristics.  Since survival depends so much on
having foo-resistance, it's useful for the bot to know that if he
comes across a winter wolf cub and doesn't have cold resistance, he
might want to get it.  Similarly, if the bot kills such a critter, it
should know that it can get the intrinsic by eating the corpse, it
could gain the resistance, but should also know that it might not want
to kill the wolf cub until it can eat it safely so it doesn't get
tainted meat sickness.

Identification is the other main thing that I try to accomplish.  My
normal strategy is to try to find the proper scroll in a shop, bless
it if possible, and then go to town on the items in my inventory. 
Some means of telling the bot to not try on cursed rings, armor, or
wielding cursed weapons would be useful.  As well as trying to let it
identify through pets or altar the b/u/c state.  These would be things
I believe would be central to the bot's early activity.

What about the feasibility of "recording" a set of games that end in
various results and playing them back for a bot to let them see what
happens with human players so the bot can see what works and what
doesn't.  By the way, is this/can this be done?  Sort of a "replay" of
games, so other hackers can see how others play the game?  I've never
found other people besides one guy who play it, so I don't know what
the really skilled players do.  This would be interesting to see.

